+/// Is the call erased when the program is compiled?
+/// This is independent of whether we erase it for the purpose of lifetime checking:
+/// e.g., a call to a proof function is kept for lifetime checking (NodeErase::Keep),
+/// but it is nonetheless erased from the compiled program.
+/// A call that is erased from the compiled program can never actually diverge at
+/// run time, even if its return type is uninhabited (e.g., `!`), so MIR building
+/// must not prune the control flow that follows it; otherwise the code after the
+/// call would be exempted from borrow-checking even though it really runs.
+/// (See https://github.com/verus-lang/verus/issues/2219)
+#[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Clone, Copy)]
+pub enum CallRuntimeErase {
+    /// The call exists in the compiled program; if its return type is uninhabited,
+    /// it really diverges and the code after it is unreachable (as in ordinary Rust).
+    Keep,
+    /// The call is ghost code, erased from the compiled program; the code after it
+    /// is reachable even if the call's return type is uninhabited.
+    Erase,
+}
+
